            "A Bill of Resources must be clearly distinguished from a standard Bill of Materials (BOM). While a Bill of Materials specifies the physical ingredients, raw components, and sub-assemblies required to manufacture one unit of a finished product, a Bill of Resources catalogs the standard run hours, setup times, direct labor hours, and specialized tooling allocations consumed by that product family across critical bottleneck work centers. Because RCCP operates at an aggregate tactical level, Bills of Resources are typically formulated for representative product families rather than individual, microscopic finished-good SKUs. This aggregation drastically reduces computational complexity while preserving the statistical fidelity necessary for medium-term capacity decisions.",
            "Three distinct mathematical methods are deployed in industrial practice to construct Bills of Resources for RCCP: the Capacity Planning using Overall Factors (CPOF) method, the Capacity Bills (or Bill of Resources) method, and the Resource Profile method. Capacity Planning using Overall Factors represents the simplest approach: it takes the total historical plant labor or machine hours required to produce an aggregate product family and allocates that total across individual work centers based on fixed historical percentage ratios. For instance, if historical data reveals that forty percent of total labor hours are consumed in Machining, thirty-five percent in Welding, and twenty-five percent in Final Assembly, CPOF applies these static percentages to any future MPS volume. While computationally effortless, CPOF suffers from severe limitations: it ignores dynamic product mix shifts within the family and completely disregards operation lead times, assuming all capacity is consumed simultaneously.",
